- max_selected_elements([1, 4, 7, 10])
- max_selected_elements([5])
- max_selected_elements([1, 1, 1])
- max_selected_elements([3, 3, 3, 3])
- max_selected_elements([i * i % 997 + 1 for i in range(1000)])
fail[2]: The numbers are 3 apart, and raising one by 1 can't close that: 1.
fail[3]: One number: 1.
fail[4]: Three 1s: keep one, raise another to 2, and the third has nowhere to go: 2.
fail[5]: Four 3s can only make 3 and 4: 2.
fail[6]: Sorted, each number either stays as x or becomes x + 1, and extends the best run ending at x − 1 or at x. The longest run here is 32.
edges:
- a single number :: len(nums) == 1 :: max_selected_elements([8])
- every number the same :: len(nums) > 1 and len(set(nums)) == 1 :: max_selected_elements([2, 2, 2])
- a gap of 2 that raising can close :: len(set(nums)) > 1 and any(x + 2 in nums for x in nums) and max_selected_elements(nums) > 1 :: max_selected_elements([1, 3, 3])
edge_count: 2
complexity: time=O(n log n) | space=O(n)
complexity_note: n is the length of `nums`.
why_time: One sort, then one pass with two dictionary lookups for each number.
why_space: The sorted copy, and the best run for each value seen.
hint: Sort the numbers. Then a run of consecutive values is built from the left, and each number can only extend a run that ends at the value right before the one it takes.
hint: Keep `best[v]`, the longest run ending at value v so far. A number x can become x + 1, extending the run ending at x, or stay as x, extending the run ending at x − 1.
hint: Update x + 1 first, using `best[x]` from before this number: `best[x + 1] = best.get(x, 0) + 1`, then `best[x] = best.get(x - 1, 0) + 1`. The answer is the biggest value you store.
right: Sorting lets each run grow from the left one number at a time, so one dictionary pass finds the longest: O(n log n), instead of trying every choice of which numbers to raise.
wrong: For x in `sorted(nums)`: `best[x + 1] = best.get(x, 0) + 1`, then `best[x] = best.get(x - 1, 0) + 1`, keeping the biggest. Return it.
